The Collector system needs some changes and polish:

The Test2::Harness2::Collector library should be a polished interface

    use Test2::Harness2::Collector qw/collect spawn_collector/;

    # Fork a new process that is a collector and will start its child
    my $pid = spawn_collector(
        env => \%env,

        exec => \@exec,         # start child and call exec(@exec)
        # or
        run  => sub { ... },    # start child and run the sub, no exec

        parser    => $parser_info,       # Class, or [$class, %construction_args], or blessed instance
        processor => $processor_info,    # Class, or [$class, %construction_args], or blessed instance
        recorder  => $recorder_info,     # Class, or [$class, %construction_args], or blessed instance

        # See existing timeout params
        %timeout_params,
    );

    # Start a new child process and collect it in this process, return when child exits.
    my $info = collect(
        # Same args
    );

    $info = {
        exit => {
            code => $INT,
            err  => $INT,
            sig  => $INT,
        },
        # Any data the processor or recorder want to attach
    }


Collector works like it does today, atomic::pipes with zst compression for
childs stderr/stdout, parsers parse the childs output into events, events are
passed to an optional processor (Processor is a role, takes input events one at
a time, returns 1 or more events for each 1 it reads). Events are then passed
to an optional recorder which may or may not act on each event passed to it.
The collector loops over output and also watches for child exit, and sends the
child exit as an event to the pipeline.

Initial recorder writes all events to a file provided via construction
arguments, jsonl.zst format, but recorder does not enforce anything about
filename, it just gets a filename and creates+write to it.

A test subclass of the recorder will also write 2 additional files, a
transitions file that gets a zst compressed json line written for each of the
transitions we care about, these will be noticed by the auditor and sent as
additional events to the recorder, the recorder sees these transition events
and writes them to the transitions file instead of the events file. The other
additional file is a 'state' file where the final state from the auditor is
written. Note that we need to be cautious about ordering, we need to make sure
the exit event from the child exiting comes AFTER all output, so either after
the pipes are both closed, or after they re forcefully closed due to the
timeouts.

The base recorder class should also accept an optional 'touchfile' argument.
This file should be touched to have its mtime modified when the collector
exits, the test recorder can also touch it every transition. This will be used
to wake up things that monitor collectors by touching it and trigerring inotify
events.

Lets also create a t2h2_collector script that takes a test file and an output
dir as arguments, the script will then fill out the rest like output filenames
(in output dir), parser, processor (auditor), etc. It will exit 0 on test pass,
and 1 on failure. The script process itself uses collect() with exec to spawn
the child process. 2 processes total.

Tests can validate other paths like spawn_collector, touch file, non-test
recorder, etc.
